  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThere is a low to medium chance for patchy fog to develop over South \nTexas today, visibilities generally between 1-4 miles as saturated \ngrounds combine with low-level saturation, low clouds, and light \nwinds. The NBM has been too warm with temperatures lately, a\nconsensus of the short range ensembles seem to perform well and\nhave been used for the temperatures through Thursday.\n\nThe mid-level trough over our area the past couple of days will \nfinally slide off to the east today, leading to rain-free conditions \nlater this afternoon through Friday. Skies will remain mostly \ncloudy to overcast into Thursday, then clear out quickly Thursday \nnight into Friday as a mid-level ridge axis centers itself over \nTexas behind a cold front. Low temperatures in the 50s are \nexpected areawide Thursday night.\n\nLow-level onshore flow returns Friday night, increasing moisture \nadvection, bringing PWAT values to around 1.50\" by Saturday, just in \ntime for a negatively tilted mid-level trough to push into South \nTexas bringing plenty of lift support with PVA on top of surface \ncoastal troughing. Medium shower and thunderstorm chances (30-50%) \nreturn Saturday into Sunday morning. The latest deterministic run of \nthe GFS is very bullish with rainfall, but ensembles between the \nGEFS, ECENS, and GEPS are in fairly good agreement with rainfall \nless than an inch during the weekend; mainly due to the fast \nprogression of the trough, but we will need to continue to monitor \nfor any progression changes in future updates. Another cold front \nlooks to push through early next week, bringing rain-free conditions \nbehind it.",
